**Lemma 5.2 (one excess atom, uniformly over all actual posteriors).** For every $r\in[1/3,2/5]$ and $q\in[d,u]$, the sole atom where $Q_q$ exceeds $P_{p,r}$ is $\beta\beta$; the sole excess atom of $V$ over $P_{\beta,r}$ is $\beta$. These sign statements hold also against every actual posterior mixture over the supported source depths.

**Proof.** $a=7/256<2/9\le a_r$. The atom $\alpha$ has $q\le1/8<1/3\le r$. At $\beta\beta$, $(1-q)(1-v)\ge g=217/256>4/9\ge(1-r)^2$. At $j=1$ the other two predicted masses are bounded above by $vu=1/256$ and $vg=217/8192$. The corresponding true masses have lower bounds $r a_r\ge2/27$ and $(1-r)^2a_r\ge2/25$. Both inequalities are strict. For all $j\ge1$, multiplying the prediction/true ratio by $(a/a_r)^{j-1}\le1$ retains strictness. At the $\beta$ atom, $1-v=31/32>2/3\ge1-r$. The remaining two families have coefficients $vu<1/9\le r^2$ and $vg<3/25\le r(1-r)^2$; again $a/a_r<1$ preserves the signs at every index. Noncompletion has mass zero under both laws. Since each sign is the same for every depth, convex mixing with any *actual* posterior preserves it. This proof covers the infinite tail; a finite number of tested atoms is not used to infer it. ∎

